 == Releasing the Plugin
 
-. Choose the commit that's to be released.
-
-. For `X.Y.0` versions.
-
-.. Create and push a `release-X.Y` branch.
-
-.. Create a Git tag in the format `vX.Y.Z`. This tag should match the value of
-`main.Version` for the commit that was chosen.
-
-. Push the `vX.Y.Z` Git tag to the remote.
-
-. Monitor the `Release` GitHub Actions workflow to ensure the plugin has been
-successfully built and released.
-
-. Update the release notes for the GitHub release by clearing them out and
-clicking the *Generate release notes* button.
-
-On both the `release-X.Y` and default branches, push the following changes.
-
-.. Update the `main.Version` value in `main.go`.
-
-.. Update the `version = ">= X.Y.Z"` version constraint in `docs/README.md`, and
-`example/template.pkr.hcl` to use the next development version.
-
-.. Run `make generate` to generate updated documentation.
+. Confirm the commit to release has passed the `build & test` workflow. For an
+`X.Y.0` release, create and push the `release-X.Y` branch before publishing.
+
+. In GitHub Actions, run the `Release` workflow with the `X.Y.Z` version (without
+a `v` prefix) and the full 40-character commit SHA. Do not create the tag
+yourself.
+
+The workflow checks out that exact commit and creates `vX.Y.Z` only in its local
+checkout. GoReleaser then runs the tests, lint, generated-file checks, workflow
+validation, and all cross-platform builds without publishing anything. Only if
+every check and build succeeds does the workflow create the remote immutable
+tag, upload the already-built artifacts to a draft GitHub release, publish the
+release, and notify HashiCorp.
+
+If a build fails, fix the source and dispatch the workflow again with the same
+version and the new commit SHA; no remote tag has been created. If publication
+fails after the remote tag or draft release is created, rerun with the same
+version and commit. The workflow verifies that the immutable tag still points
+to that commit and replaces assets on the draft before publishing it. A release
+that GitHub has already published cannot be rebuilt or changed.
+
+If only the HashiCorp notification must be retried, run the
+`HashiCorp Integration Release (Manual)` workflow with the `X.Y.Z` version. It
+resolves the immutable final tag and requires an existing published GitHub
+release before notifying HashiCorp. It does not publish artifacts.
+
+There is no next-release version to maintain in `main.go`. Go 1.27 derives the
+main module version from the repository: a build at a release tag reports
+`X.Y.Z`, while later commits receive a Go pseudo-version based on that tag,
+timestamp, and revision. Because Packer accepts only `dev` as a development
+prerelease, the plugin exposes those commits as
+`X.Y.Z-dev+<pseudo-version-details>` and adds `.dirty` for modified trees. The
+original Go pseudo-version remains in the binary's embedded build information.
+The workflow's local final tag makes every GoReleaser binary report exactly
+`X.Y.Z` without linker-injected version variables. Update documented Packer
+version constraints when a new release should become the minimum, and run
+`make generate` to commit any resulting generated documentation before release.
+
+Repository administrators must allow GitHub Actions to use the read/write
+`GITHUB_TOKEN` requested by the release workflow. No long-lived release token is
+stored. They must also configure a tag ruleset for `v*` that prevents tag
+updates and deletions while permitting the release workflow to create tags,
+with mutation access limited to an explicit break-glass role.
+Release checksums are not signed until
+https://github.com/oxidecomputer/packer-plugin-oxide/issues/26[issue #26] is
+resolved.
